it's the british navy's biggest  and most powerful warship the hms queen  elizabeth  aircraft carrier is leading a flotilla  of navy ships  on its maiden deployment it's the  largest  concentration of british air and  maritime power in years  prime minister boris johnson says the  deployment will help to strengthen  british soft power such as belief in  democracy  and the rule of law analysts say  geopolitics is at play it's also doing  it because there are tensions over  taiwan  and britain like the eu and other major  powers  have an interest in not seeing that  become an armed conflict  and so they have to demonstrate that  their country is willing to stand up  against the use of force against it and  i also feel  it's it's an early signal to the biden  administration that britain  is a player an ally no longer trapped in  europe but able to act  independently the flotilla is expected  to sail through the south china sea  a large part of which china has laid  claim to  part of its mission is to demonstrate  freedom of navigation  in the south china sea but the chinese  government  sees this as a provocation in recent  years  it has increased its military spending  and grown more assertive in its claims  over the south china sea  tensions are already heightened since  2015 uh when china began  island building activities in the south  china sea but what i'm worried now is  that with the collection of  so many military assets in this region  there's going to be  the higher chance of miscalculation the  uk  recently announced it will permanently  deploy two warships in  asian waters after the flotilla sails to  japan  in september the deployment is part of  the british government's tilt towards  the indo-pacific  much like the u.s pivot to asia under  former u.s president barack obama  it is in part a response to china's  growing influence  and that makes the south china sea more  of a potential flash point  florence louis al jazeera kuala lumpur
 

